WEBVTT

00:00.490 --> 00:01.990
What I want to do
first is just show

00:01.990 --> 00:05.100
you what the angle
bisector theorem is

00:05.100 --> 00:07.520
and then we'll actually
prove it for ourselves.

00:07.520 --> 00:10.490
So I just have an
arbitrary triangle right

00:10.490 --> 00:12.180
over here, triangle ABC.

00:12.180 --> 00:13.750
And what I'm going
to do is I'm going

00:13.750 --> 00:17.249
to draw an angle bisector
for this angle up here.

00:17.249 --> 00:19.540
And we could have done it
with any of the three angles,

00:19.540 --> 00:20.270
but I'll just do this one.

00:20.270 --> 00:22.290
I'll make our proof
a little bit easier.

00:22.290 --> 00:25.670
So I'm just going to bisect
this angle, angle ABC.

00:25.670 --> 00:29.900
So let's just say that's the
angle bisector of angle ABC,

00:29.900 --> 00:31.950
and so this angle
right over here

00:31.950 --> 00:35.040
is equal to this
angle right over here.

00:35.040 --> 00:38.700
And let me call this point down
here-- let me call it point D.

00:38.700 --> 00:47.560
The angle bisector
theorem tells us

00:47.560 --> 00:50.290
that the ratio between
the sides that aren't this

00:50.290 --> 00:52.680
bisector-- so when I put
this angle bisector here,

00:52.680 --> 00:55.750
it created two smaller triangles
out of that larger one.

00:55.750 --> 00:57.410
The angle bisector
theorem tells us

00:57.410 --> 01:01.360
the ratios between the other
sides of these two triangles

01:01.360 --> 01:04.090
that we've now created
are going to be the same.

01:04.090 --> 01:12.620
So it tells us that
the ratio of AB to AD

01:12.620 --> 01:19.310
is going to be equal to the
ratio of BC to, you could say,

01:19.310 --> 01:21.820
CD.

01:21.820 --> 01:24.820
So the ratio of--
I'll color code it.

01:24.820 --> 01:32.310
The ratio of that,
which is this, to this

01:32.310 --> 01:36.590
is going to be equal to
the ratio of this, which

01:36.590 --> 01:40.970
is that, to this right
over here-- to CD,

01:40.970 --> 01:42.020
which is that over here.

01:42.020 --> 01:43.799
So once you see the
ratio of that to that,

01:43.799 --> 01:46.090
it's going to be the same as
the ratio of that to that.

01:46.090 --> 01:48.214
So that's kind of a cool
result, but you can't just

01:48.214 --> 01:50.680
accept it on faith because
it's a cool result.

01:50.680 --> 01:52.900
You want to prove
it to ourselves.

01:52.900 --> 01:54.620
And so you can imagine
right over here,

01:54.620 --> 01:56.140
we have some ratios set up.

01:56.140 --> 01:58.920
So we're going to prove it
using similar triangles.

01:58.920 --> 02:02.120
And unfortunate for us, these
two triangles right here

02:02.120 --> 02:04.599
aren't necessarily similar.

02:04.599 --> 02:07.015
We know that these two angles
are congruent to each other,

02:07.015 --> 02:08.490
but we don't know
whether this angle is

02:08.490 --> 02:09.735
equal to that angle
or that angle.

02:09.735 --> 02:10.318
We don't know.

02:10.318 --> 02:12.240
We can't make any
statements like that.

02:12.240 --> 02:14.690
So in order to actually set
up this type of a statement,

02:14.690 --> 02:17.310
we'll have to construct
maybe another triangle that

02:17.310 --> 02:20.440
will be similar to one
of these right over here.

02:20.440 --> 02:27.060
And one way to do it would
be to draw another line.

02:27.060 --> 02:29.545
And this proof
wasn't obvious to me

02:29.545 --> 02:31.170
the first time that
I thought about it,

02:31.170 --> 02:33.350
so don't worry if it's
not obvious to you.

02:33.350 --> 02:37.130
What happens is if we can
continue this bisector--

02:37.130 --> 02:38.830
this angle bisector
right over here,

02:38.830 --> 02:40.030
so let's just continue it.

02:40.030 --> 02:42.970
It just keeps going
on and on and on.

02:42.970 --> 02:45.860
And let's also-- maybe we can
construct a similar triangle

02:45.860 --> 02:47.910
to this triangle
over here if we draw

02:47.910 --> 02:51.660
a line that's parallel
to AB down here.

02:51.660 --> 02:53.070
So let's try to do that.

02:53.070 --> 02:57.070
So I'm just going to say,
well, if C is not on AB,

02:57.070 --> 03:00.080
you could always find
a point or a line that

03:00.080 --> 03:02.920
goes through C that
is parallel to AB.

03:02.920 --> 03:05.500
So by definition, let's
just create another line

03:05.500 --> 03:07.190
right over here.

03:07.190 --> 03:10.770
And let's call this
point right over here F

03:10.770 --> 03:13.990
and let's just pick
this line in such a way

03:13.990 --> 03:16.570
that FC is parallel to AB.

03:16.570 --> 03:21.120
So this is parallel to
that right over there.

03:24.520 --> 03:26.550
And we could just
construct it that way.

03:26.550 --> 03:28.240
And now we have some
interesting things.

03:28.240 --> 03:29.614
And we did it that
way so that we

03:29.614 --> 03:32.680
can make these two triangles
be similar to each other.

03:32.680 --> 03:33.622
So let's see that.

03:33.622 --> 03:34.580
Let's see what happens.

03:34.580 --> 03:36.288
So before we even
think about similarity,

03:36.288 --> 03:40.640
let's think about what we know
about some of the angles here.

03:40.640 --> 03:44.160
We know that we have
alternate interior angles-- so

03:44.160 --> 03:45.910
just think about these
two parallel lines.

03:45.910 --> 03:48.790
So I could imagine AB
keeps going like that.

03:48.790 --> 03:50.840
FC keeps going like that.

03:50.840 --> 03:54.000
And line BD right
here is a transversal.

03:54.000 --> 03:57.580
Then whatever this
angle is, this angle

03:57.580 --> 04:00.226
is going to be as well, from
alternate interior angles,

04:00.226 --> 04:02.350
which we've talked a lot
about when we first talked

04:02.350 --> 04:05.330
about angles with
transversals and all of that.

04:05.330 --> 04:07.540
So these two angles are
going to be the same.

04:07.540 --> 04:09.160
But this angle and
this angle are also

04:09.160 --> 04:11.250
going to be the same, because
this angle and that angle

04:11.250 --> 04:11.791
are the same.

04:11.791 --> 04:12.880
This is a bisector.

04:12.880 --> 04:14.790
Because this is a
bisector, we know

04:14.790 --> 04:18.790
that angle ABD is the
same as angle DBC.

04:18.790 --> 04:20.730
So whatever this angle
is, that angle is.

04:20.730 --> 04:22.600
And so is this angle.

04:22.600 --> 04:25.490
And that gives us kind
of an interesting result,

04:25.490 --> 04:29.180
because here we have
a situation where

04:29.180 --> 04:32.400
if you look at this
larger triangle BFC,

04:32.400 --> 04:34.990
we have two base angles
that are the same, which

04:34.990 --> 04:37.290
means this must be an
isosceles triangle.

04:37.290 --> 04:39.925
So BC must be the same as FC.

04:43.510 --> 04:44.670
So that was kind of cool.

04:44.670 --> 04:47.341
We just used the transversal and
the alternate interior angles

04:47.341 --> 04:48.715
to show that these
are isosceles,

04:48.715 --> 04:51.690
and that BC and FC
are the same thing.

04:51.690 --> 04:56.460
And that could be
useful, because we

04:56.460 --> 04:58.890
have a feeling that this
triangle and this triangle

04:58.890 --> 04:59.890
are going to be similar.

04:59.890 --> 05:01.050
We haven't proven it yet.

05:01.050 --> 05:04.520
But how will that help us get
something about BC up here?

05:04.520 --> 05:08.110
But we just showed that BC
and FC are the same thing.

05:08.110 --> 05:09.730
So this is going to
be the same thing.

05:09.730 --> 05:11.140
If we want to
prove it, if we can

05:11.140 --> 05:14.420
prove that the ratio of
AB to AD is the same thing

05:14.420 --> 05:17.240
as the ratio of FC
to CD, we're going

05:17.240 --> 05:22.240
to be there because BC, we
just showed, is equal to FC.

05:22.240 --> 05:24.030
But let's not start
with the theorem.

05:24.030 --> 05:25.446
Let's actually get
to the theorem.

05:25.446 --> 05:27.290
So FC is parallel
to AB, [? able ?]

05:27.290 --> 05:30.910
to set up this one
isosceles triangle,

05:30.910 --> 05:32.289
so these sides are congruent.

05:32.289 --> 05:34.080
Now, let's look at some
of the other angles

05:34.080 --> 05:37.180
here and make ourselves
feel good about it.

05:37.180 --> 05:38.320
Well, we have this.

05:38.320 --> 05:41.450
If we look at triangle ABD, so
this triangle right over here,

05:41.450 --> 05:44.160
and triangle FDC, we
already established

05:44.160 --> 05:46.930
that they have one set of
angles that are the same.

05:46.930 --> 05:49.310
And then, and then
they also both-- ABD

05:49.310 --> 05:51.460
has this angle right
over here, which

05:51.460 --> 05:53.630
is a vertical angle
with this one over here,

05:53.630 --> 05:54.880
so they're congruent.

05:54.880 --> 05:57.490
And we know if two triangles
have two angles that

05:57.490 --> 05:59.170
are the same, actually
the third one's

05:59.170 --> 06:00.380
going to be the same as well.

06:00.380 --> 06:02.560
Or you could say by the
angle-angle similarity

06:02.560 --> 06:05.917
postulate, these two
triangles are similar.

06:05.917 --> 06:07.000
So let me write that down.

06:07.000 --> 06:09.650
You want to make sure you get
the corresponding sides right.

06:09.650 --> 06:11.382
We now know by
angle-angle-- and I'm

06:11.382 --> 06:12.840
going to start at
the green angle--

06:12.840 --> 06:18.530
that triangle B-- and
then the blue angle-- BDA

06:18.530 --> 06:23.130
is similar to triangle--
so then once again,

06:23.130 --> 06:25.270
let's start with the
green angle, F. Then,

06:25.270 --> 06:26.652
you go to the blue angle, FDC.

06:31.030 --> 06:33.640
And here, we want to eventually
get to the angle bisector

06:33.640 --> 06:39.090
theorem, so we want to look at
the ratio between AB and AD.

06:39.090 --> 06:40.740
Similar triangles,
either you could

06:40.740 --> 06:42.820
find the ratio between
corresponding sides

06:42.820 --> 06:45.890
are going to be
similar triangles,

06:45.890 --> 06:48.040
or you could find
the ratio between two

06:48.040 --> 06:50.630
sides of a similar triangle
and compare them to the ratio

06:50.630 --> 06:53.820
the same two corresponding sides
on the other similar triangle,

06:53.820 --> 06:55.052
and they should be the same.

06:55.052 --> 06:57.010
So by similar triangles,
we know that the ratio

06:57.010 --> 07:03.130
of AB-- and this, by the way,
was by angle-angle similarity.

07:03.130 --> 07:04.980
Want to write that down.

07:04.980 --> 07:06.550
So now that we know
they're similar,

07:06.550 --> 07:12.717
we know the ratio of AB to
AD is going to be equal to--

07:12.717 --> 07:15.050
and we could even look here
for the corresponding sides.

07:15.050 --> 07:17.610
The ratio of AB, the
corresponding side

07:17.610 --> 07:23.553
is going to be CF-- is
going to equal CF over AD.

07:23.553 --> 07:28.120
AD is the same thing
as CD-- over CD.

07:28.120 --> 07:32.680
And so we know the ratio of AB
to AD is equal to CF over CD.

07:32.680 --> 07:34.790
But we just proved to
ourselves, because this

07:34.790 --> 07:38.495
is an isosceles triangle, that
CF is the same thing as BC

07:38.495 --> 07:39.470
right over here.

07:42.750 --> 07:43.760
And we're done.

07:43.760 --> 07:48.030
We've just proven AB over
AD is equal to BC over CD.

07:48.030 --> 07:51.460
So there's two things we
had to do here is one,

07:51.460 --> 07:54.930
construct this other
triangle, that, assuming

07:54.930 --> 07:58.220
this was parallel, that gave
us two things, that gave us

07:58.220 --> 08:01.010
another angle to show
that they're similar

08:01.010 --> 08:02.820
and also allowed us
to establish-- sorry,

08:02.820 --> 08:05.520
I have something
stuck in my throat.

08:05.520 --> 08:06.710
Just coughed off camera.

08:06.710 --> 08:10.630
So I should go get a
drink of water after this.

08:10.630 --> 08:13.090
So constructing
this triangle here,

08:13.090 --> 08:15.080
we were able to both
show it's similar

08:15.080 --> 08:17.660
and to construct this
larger isosceles triangle

08:17.660 --> 08:24.380
to show, look, if we can
find the ratio of this side

08:24.380 --> 08:26.670
to this side is the same
as a ratio of this side

08:26.670 --> 08:28.710
to this side, that's
analogous to showing

08:28.710 --> 08:31.510
that the ratio of this side
to this side is the same as BC

08:31.510 --> 08:32.429
to CD.

08:32.429 --> 08:34.709
And we are done.
